issue.description:
Refine the App shells preview so the shell examples feel closer to real-world desktop application proportions and the Inset project better matches the restrained shadcn inset-sidebar visual language.

## Overall direction

The current App shell previews feel too small and compressed relative to how the same shell architecture would look in a real product.

Goals:

* make the shell previews substantially larger within the available preview area;
* preserve their balanced landscape proportions;
* increase sidebar and topbar/header dimensions so the shells resemble realistic desktop app chrome;
* refine the Inset project shell so the sidebar and connected outer shell body use a muted surface;
* reduce the visual intensity of the Inset project right/main panel shadow;
* keep the result polished, realistic, and useful as a showcase rather than a miniature mockup.

Reference direction for the Inset project:
[https://ui.shadcn.com/blocks](<https://ui.shadcn.com/blocks>)

Use the shadcn inset/sidebar block examples as a visual reference for structure and restraint, without copying unrelated block content.

## 1. Increase App shell preview size

Increase the desktop width of each App shell preview to approximately **90% of the available preview/container width**.

Requirements:

* treat 90% as a visual target rather than a brittle exact percentage;
* tune the final width through browser evidence;
* preserve the current vertical stacked sequence;
* keep all shell examples consistently sized where practical;
* allow narrow-width layouts to approach full width as needed for responsive containment;
* do not introduce page-level horizontal overflow.

## 2. Preserve balanced proportions

As shell width increases, increase height proportionally so the previews remain visually balanced and landscape-oriented.

Requirements:

* do not simply stretch width while leaving height unchanged;
* preserve a realistic desktop app-window proportion;
* avoid excessive vertical height;
* tune final dimensions through browser evidence;
* keep the same overall shell structure and content hierarchy.

The goal is to make the shells feel like believable app screenshots/previews rather than reduced thumbnails.

## 3. Increase sidebar and topbar/header realism

The current shell sidebars and topbars/headers feel undersized relative to real-world desktop app chrome.

Increase these dimensions so they better resemble typical production UI proportions.

Adjust where appropriate:

* sidebar width;
* topbar/header height;
* spacing/padding within header and sidebar chrome;
* navigation row sizing only if necessary to preserve realistic rhythm.

Requirements:

* preserve the existing shadcn Sidebar architecture;
* preserve current nav/content structure;
* preserve icons, labels, active states, and footer/profile treatment;
* do not redesign sidebar content;
* do not create new shell-specific sizing systems if existing accepted sidebar/header sizing can be reused;
* keep the three shell examples visually coherent.

The intent is realistic scale, not oversized controls.

## 4. Inset project muted outer shell

For the **Inset project** example, use the semantic **muted** surface for the sidebar background and the connected outer shell body surrounding the inset right/main panel.

Conceptually:

* sidebar area → muted surface;
* connected shell body/background around the main panel → same muted surface;
* right/main inset panel → remains the distinct content surface.

Requirements:

* use semantic theme tokens rather than hard-coded colors;
* preserve live light/dark behavior;
* preserve Sidebar token behavior where applicable;
* maintain clear separation between outer shell and inset content panel;
* keep the result visually connected so sidebar + outer body read as one shell surface.

Do not apply this muted outer-shell treatment to the other App shell examples unless source inspection shows it is already part of their accepted design.

## 5. Reduce Inset project right-panel shadow

The current right/main panel shadow on Inset project is visually too strong.

Reduce the elevation so it better matches the restrained treatment of shadcn's inset/sidebar block examples.

Direction:

* use subtle separation rather than dramatic floating elevation;
* prefer an existing accepted semantic shadow token/class;
* avoid one-off raw shadow values unless the current accepted system has no suitable equivalent;
* preserve the panel border/radius and inset relationship;
* keep light/dark behavior coherent.

Do not blindly copy a shadow value from the reference page. Match the restrained visual character using the existing Personal UI shadow system.

## 6. Preserve shell behavior

Do not change:

* App shell order;
* Classic workspace / Inset project / Compact utility identities;
* sidebar collapse behavior;
* preview-local collapse isolation;
* Workbench sidebar isolation;
* active navigation behavior;
* workspace switchers;
* abstract main-panel content structure;
* Theme v3;
* UI Effects semantics;
* persistence/history;
* Design Command;
* mutation operations;
* public registry.

No new dependency or network behavior.

## 7. Accessibility and responsiveness

Preserve:

* keyboard/focus behavior for sidebar controls;
* icon-only collapsed-state accessibility;
* responsive containment;
* logical reading order;
* narrow-width usability;
* light/dark contrast;
* no horizontal page overflow.

Larger shell geometry must not regress collapsed sidebar behavior or preview isolation.

## Acceptance

* App shell previews occupy roughly 90% of the available desktop preview width, tuned through browser evidence.
* Shell heights increase proportionally so the previews remain balanced and landscape-oriented.
* Sidebar width and topbar/header height feel closer to realistic production desktop proportions.
* Shells no longer read as miniature/compressed mockups.
* Inset project sidebar uses the semantic muted surface.
* Inset project's connected outer shell body uses the same muted surface.
* Inset project's right/main panel remains visually distinct as the inset content surface.
* Inset project right-panel shadow is noticeably more restrained and closer in character to shadcn inset/sidebar block examples.
* Existing sidebar architecture, collapse behavior, isolation, navigation, theme linkage, responsiveness, and accessibility remain intact.
* No Theme v3, UI Effects, persistence/history, Design Command, mutation, registry, dependency, or network regression is introduced.
